steps.body:
`condition ? a : b` evaluates the condition. If it is true the whole expression is `a`, otherwise it is `b`. Only the chosen branch runs, so `count != 0 ? total / count : 0` never divides by zero. It produces a value, so it fits where an if/else cannot, like the right side of an assignment.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int x = 14;
        int y = 9;
        int score = 72;
        {{code}}
        System.out.println("Larger: " + larger);
        System.out.println("Label: " + label);
    }
}
```

```java
int larger = x > y ? x : y;
String label = score >= 50 ? "PASS" : "FAIL";
```

Inside a concatenation, wrap the ternary in parentheses. Without them, `+` binds tighter than `==`, so `n + " " + n == 1 ? ...` tries to compare a String with an int and does not compile.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int n = 3;
        {{code}}
    }
}
```

```java
System.out.println(n + " " + (n == 1 ? "item" : "items"));
```

The whole ternary has one type, so both branches must fit where you use it. `int n = isWhole ? 1 : "one";` is a compile error because `"one"` is not an `int`. Keep both branches the same type. When the branches are two different number types, Java promotes them to the wider one, so `isWhole ? 1 : 2.5` is a `double` and prints `1.0` even when `isWhole` is true.

Use a ternary to choose a value, and if/else to run different actions.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int cartCount = 1;
        int bagCount = 3;
        System.out.println(cartCount + " " + (cartCount == 1 ? "item" : "items"));
        System.out.println(bagCount + " " + (bagCount == 1 ? "item" : "items"));

        int left = 14;
        int right = 9;
        int larger = left > right ? left : right;
        System.out.println("Larger: " + larger);

        boolean isWhole = true;
        System.out.println(isWhole ? 1 : 2.5);
    }
}

