public class Main {
    static final int PASS_MARK = 50;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int reportCount = 1;
        int photoCount = 7;
        int mathScore = 64;
        int artScore = 38;

        System.out.println(reportCount + " " + (reportCount == 1 ? "file" : "files"));
        System.out.println(photoCount + " " + (photoCount == 1 ? "file" : "files"));

        String mathLabel = mathScore >= PASS_MARK ? "PASS" : "FAIL";
        String artLabel = artScore >= PASS_MARK ? "PASS" : "FAIL";
        System.out.println("Math: " + mathLabel);
        System.out.println("Art: " + artLabel);
    }
}

steps.body:
For three outcomes, put a second ternary in the false branch of the first. The ternary groups from the right, so the chain reads like if, else if, else: the first true condition wins and the last value is the fallback.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int n = 42;
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(n + " is " + size);
    }
}
```

```java
String size = n < 10 ? "small"
        : n < 100 ? "medium"
        : "large";
```

Put each condition on its own line so the chain stays readable. Order the conditions the same way you would order else if branches, most specific first. With `<` tests that means the smallest threshold first, because `n < 100` is also true for 5.

Stop at about three outcomes. Past that, or when a branch needs more than one statement, an if/else chain is clearer. When you match exact values instead of ranges, a switch expression is clearer still. Here is the same choice as an if/else chain:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int n = 42;
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(n + " is " + size);
    }
}
```

```java
String size;
if (n < 10) {
    size = "small";
} else if (n < 100) {
    size = "medium";
} else {
    size = "large";
}
```

Avoid nesting a ternary inside the *true* branch as well. It compiles, but it is very hard to read.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int first = -4;
        int second = 0;
        int third = 9;

        String firstSign = first < 0 ? "negative"
                : first == 0 ? "zero"
                : "positive";
        String secondSign = second < 0 ? "negative"
                : second == 0 ? "zero"
                : "positive";
        String thirdSign = third < 0 ? "negative"
                : third == 0 ? "zero"
                : "positive";

        System.out.println(first + " is " + firstSign);
        System.out.println(second + " is " + secondSign);
        System.out.println(third + " is " + thirdSign);
    }
}

